Z||000008||terryk||06-17-2000||12:59 AM||||More fuel is not the answer unless it is matched with more air (as ThatRickGuy pointed out).<P>Put a scanner on the ECM and read the Block Learn Multiplier, Integrator and O2 Volts at wide open throttle. Then watch the BLM and INT at cruise. Keep a steady foot.<P>That will tell you if more fuel is needed. Ten buck's, it is very close to right or possibly less fuel is needed at WOT.<P>Yes, larger injectors is a way to increase the fuel if you can't do it in the ECM. More pressure tends to make the injectors spit. Not good. More fuel makes the initial acceleration enrichment better but tends to make the power enrichment worse. <BR>Translation? The first stomp on the throttle feels stronger, but the actual acceleration is slower.<P>GM already runs the engines a bit rich for some safety margin and cooler cylinder temperatures. <P>Any serious tuning requires a scanner (Diacom, Xray, OTC, or one of the homemades). Otherwise you are just guessing.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 06-17-2000).]||||reg||1
